# Exclude non-app files from the static-assets bundle (honored by wrangler
# for both `wrangler dev` and `wrangler deploy`). Without this, the .git
# directory's large objects (28 MiB pack) blow past the 25 MiB per-asset
# limit and the Worker refuses to boot (verified locally: `wrangler dev`
# failed with "Asset too large." pointing at .git/objects/...).
# NOTE: everything the APP serves at runtime stays included — js/, css/,
# vendor/whisper (offline transcription), manifests, icons, and pages.
#
# Parity with the deploy script: real deploy excludes *.md and *.json then
# copies package.json + wrangler.jsonc back. We mirror that here so CI's
# dry-run validates the same asset bundle that ships to production.
.git
.wrangler
node_modules
.dev.vars
tmp
dogfood-output
serve.cjs
planners/
*.txt
qa-*.cjs
tools
migrations
*.md
# 2026-10-04: *.md does not match a backup like README.md.orig - the suffix
# is .orig, not .md - so restoring one of those files would have silently
# published it as a public static asset. Same class as the .tgz leak.
*.orig
*.head
*~
# 2026-10-04: local secret files. .dev.vars was already here, but .env*,
# *.pem and *.key were git-ignored WITHOUT a matching asset exclude - so
# ignoring them would have made them invisible to git while still
# publishing them to the internet. Both sides must move together.
/.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
secrets.json
*.json
*.yml
*.yaml
.gitleaks.toml
# 2026-10-06 (Wave 8.2): source maps are build artifacts. dist/bundle.js.map was
# publicly served (2.4 MB of unminified source). Excluded here AND guarded in
# worker.js so neither path can leak it.
*.map
**/*.map
# 2026-10-08 (billing directive): named cookie files and the billing patch.
*cookies*
*.patch
# 2026-10-15 (W1): the backend source tree and dev tooling were PUBLICLY
# DOWNLOADABLE. .assetsignore is the filter wrangler actually honours for the
# asset upload, and src/, worker.js, test/, mcp/ and eslint.config.js were simply
# absent from it - while the three negations above (!package.json,
# !wrangler.jsonc, !skills-lock.json) forced three more files public. Verified
# live on 2026-10-15 that all of these answered 200 with real content:
# /src/lib/http.js /src/auth/session.js /src/billing.js /src/ai-proxy.js
# /src/cloud/projects.js /src/router.js /mcp/server.mjs /worker.js
# /package.json /wrangler.jsonc /eslint.config.js /test/billing.test.mjs
# /skills-lock.json
# These paths must still EXIST on disk in the deploy staging copy - wrangler
# bundles worker.js and follows its imports into src/, and it reads
# wrangler.jsonc + package.json to build at all. Excluding them from the UPLOAD
# is what is wanted; excluding them from the STAGE would break the build.
src
worker.js
build.js
test
mcp
docs
.github
.githooks
.freebuff
eslint.config.js
package-lock.json
CODEOWNERS
# robots.txt must be UPLOADED, not ignored: worker.js serves it (and
# sitemap.xml) through env.ASSETS.fetch() in its SEO_FILES branch, so '*.txt'
# above made that branch 404 forever. Without this negation the repo's
# robots.txt is dead weight and Cloudflare's zone-level file silently wins -
# verified 2026-10-15: live /robots.txt was 1248 bytes of the zone file while
# the repo's own robots.txt is 530 bytes and was never served.
!robots.txt
